#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054500 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054757 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be393dbe-03cc-5259-ba28-8f865537c57a" :knowledge-point/title "Rationalizing Denominators Containing Two Terms"}] :question/id #uuid "04b471e2-edcd-5f01-8677-e93ddc547a0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-18927" :question/problem "Rationalize the denominator of $\\dfrac {2}{\\sqrt{3} - 1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{3} + 1.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{3} + 1,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {2}{\\sqrt{3} - 1} &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {2}{\\sqrt{3} - 1} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\sqrt{3} + 1} {\\sqrt{3} + 1} &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {2\\left(\\sqrt 3 + 1\\right)} {\\left(\\sqrt{3}\\right)^2 - 1^2} &= \\[5pt]\t\n\t\t\\dfrac {2\\left(\\sqrt 3 + 1\\right)} {3 - 1} &= \\[5pt]\t\t\n\t\t\\dfrac {2\\left(\\sqrt 3 + 1\\right)} {2} &= \\[5pt]\t\n\t\t\\sqrt 3 + 1 &\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054501 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eae5693c-a37e-58f4-8793-0875ef8e0fb8" :knowledge-point/title "Rationalizing Denominators Containing Two Surds"}] :question/id #uuid "0e811568-769f-5e18-9c6c-5594c18330b5" :question/math-academy-id "e-18931" :question/problem "Rationalize the denominator of $\\dfrac{6}{\\sqrt{10} - \\sqrt{6}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{10} + \\sqrt{6}.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{10} + \\sqrt{6},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{6}} & = \\\\ \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{6}} & = \\\\ \\frac{6(\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{6})}{(\\sqrt{10})^{2}-(\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{6(\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{6})}{10-6} & = \\\\ \\frac{6(\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{6})}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{6})}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054502 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054759 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "203f17e9-3efb-5637-ac02-2c31e5a8a632" :knowledge-point/title "Rationalizing Denominators Containing Surds in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "5a664cc0-30ca-5097-9505-0755963b0f1f" :question/math-academy-id "e-18932" :question/problem "Rationalize the denominator of $\\dfrac{2 - \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2} + 1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{2} - 1.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{2} - 1,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}+1} & = \\\\ \\frac{2-\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}-1}{\\sqrt{2}-1} & = \\\\ \\frac{(2-\\sqrt{2})(\\sqrt{2}-1)}{(\\sqrt{2}+1)(\\sqrt{2}-1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-2-(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+\\sqrt{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}-(1)^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-2-2+\\sqrt{2}}{2-1} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-4+\\sqrt{2}}{1} & = \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515594 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eae5693c-a37e-58f4-8793-0875ef8e0fb8" :knowledge-point/title "Rationalizing Denominators Containing Two Surds"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14e9c5e7-56dd-5716-a24c-744b86fd4e7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}+\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514917} {:answer/id #uuid "2da510ec-5b98-588f-928e-d5c8aad57cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(\\sqrt{7}+\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514974} {:answer/id #uuid "42a587ea-a2dc-5010-8fc2-6b011658ec94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186